{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 5, :comments ["<b>And every plant of the field</b> — According to the opinion of our Sages in Bereshit Rabbah (see Ḥullin 60a), on the third day they stood at the entrance of the earth's surface, and on the sixth day they sprouted after He rained upon them. But in my opinion, according to the plain sense, on the third day the earth brought forth the grass and the fruit tree in their heights and their comeliness, as they were commanded concerning them, and now Scripture relates that no one was planting and sowing from them, and it would not sprout until a mist ascended from it and watered it, and the man was formed who would work it to sow and to plant and to guard it. And this is the meaning of \"plant of the field before it sprouted\" — it did not say \"plant of the ground,\" for the worked place is called \"field\": \"which you sow in the field\" (Exodus 23:16), \"we will not pass through field or vineyard\" (Numbers 20:17). And this is the ordinary functioning of the world that came into being after the six days of Creation onward for all the days of eternity, for by means of the mist the heavens cause rain, and by their means the earth brings forth its sowings:"], :book :genesis, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 2, :notes {0 "The citation \"(see Ḥullin 60a)\" is parenthetical in the Hebrew source text alongside the Bereshit Rabbah reference; rendered as part of the flow."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T03:23:55.253619156Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}